From: Andre Brait
Subject: [PATCH] net: phylink: reject a forced link with unknown speed or duplex

With autoneg disabled, phylink_ethtool_ksettings_set() looks up the
requested speed with an inexact phy_caps_lookup(). SPEED_UNKNOWN (-1)
never matches, so the lookup returns the slowest supported mode.

netifd reads the link settings and writes them back unchanged when a
device has pause, asym_pause, speed, duplex or autoneg set. On an SFP+
port whose link is still down, the read returns autoneg off and
SPEED_UNKNOWN, so the write-back forces 1000 Mb/s and phylink switches
a 10GBASE-R port to 1000base-x. The 10G link never comes back.

phylib already rejects this through phy_check_valid(). Do the same in
phylink and return -EINVAL.

---
--- a/drivers/net/phy/phylink.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/phylink.c
@@ -3331,8 +3331,16 @@
 	switch (kset->base.autoneg) {
 	case AUTONEG_DISABLE:
 		/* Autonegotiation disabled, select a suitable speed and
+		 * duplex.
+		 */
+		/* An inexact lookup maps SPEED_UNKNOWN to the slowest
+		 * supported mode, so a forced link needs a real speed and
 		 * duplex.
 		 */
+		if (kset->base.speed == SPEED_UNKNOWN ||
+		    kset->base.duplex == DUPLEX_UNKNOWN)
+			return -EINVAL;
+
 		c = phy_caps_lookup(kset->base.speed, kset->base.duplex,
 				    pl->supported, false);
 		if (!c)
